eck uses jl (signed less-than). Same signed/unsigned mismatch as edit_cookie. OOB read from msg array yields a pointer that is passed to printf as string, potentially leaking memory content.\"},{\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"input\":\"random_index (fread from /dev/urandom)\",\"operation\":\"free(msg[random_index]) then swap msg[cookie_num-1] -> msg[random_index] without clearing\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"After free, the freed pointer is overwritten by swap (msg[random_index] = msg[cookie_num-1]), but the old msg[cookie_num-1] slot is not zeroed. If cookie_num-1 == random_index (only one cookie), the freed pointer remains in msg[random_index]. Index is random (not attacker-controlled), limiting direct exploitation, but the swap logic may leave dangling pointers in edge cases.\"},{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"input\":\"message_size (scanf %lld)\",\"operation\":\"calloc(message_size, 1) then read(0, buf, message_size)\",\"risk\":\"LOW\",\"reason\":\"message_size checked <= 0x100 and cookie_num <= 0x1f. calloc zero-initializes. No obvious overflow. Size is user-controlled but bounded.\"},{\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"input\":\"msg_size[edit_index] (from create_cookie, user-controlled size)\",\"operation\":\"read(0, msg[edit_index], msg_size[edit_index]-1)\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"The read length is derived from msg_size which was set during create_cookie from user input (up to 0x100). If OOB index is used to read msg_size from an adjacent global variable, the read length could be controlled, amplifying the OOB write primitive.\"}],\"facts\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Binary is PIE, Full RELRO, NX, Canary, not stripped, amd64\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F002\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"libc is Ubuntu GLIBC 2.39-0ubuntu8.8 at /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6\",\"source\":\"RUNTIME\"},{\"id\":\"F003\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg is a pointer array at 0x4060, 32 elements x 8 bytes = 256 bytes in .bss\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F004\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg_size is an int32 array at 0x4160, 32 elements x 4 bytes = 128 bytes in .bss\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F005\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"cookie_num is int64 at 0x41e8 in .bss, initialized to 5 by init()\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F006\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"urandom is FILE* at 0x41e0, opened from /dev/urandom in init()\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F007\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"init() pre-creates 5 cookies via strdup with sizes 0x50,0x30,0x18,0x38,0x40 and sets cookie_num=5\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F008\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie checks cookie_num<=0x1f (jle) and message_size<=0x100 (jle), uses calloc(size,1) and read(0,buf,size)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F009\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie reads 1 byte from /dev/urandom, computes random_index = byte % cookie_num, frees msg[random_index], then swaps msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[random_index] and decrements cookie_num\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F010\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie reads index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") into uint64 at rbp-0x10, checks index < cookie_num using jl (signed less-than)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F011\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"read_cookie reads index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") into uint64 at rbp-0x10, checks index < cookie_num using jl (signed less-than)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F012\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie reads msg_size[index]-1 bytes into msg[index] via read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F013\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"read_cookie outputs msg[index] via printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[index]) with no length limit\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F014\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"GOT is writable (Full RELRO means .got.plt is merged into .got which is in RELRO segment, but .got section at 0x3f58 has WA flags)\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F015\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie and read_cookie use %llu (unsigned) for index input but jl (signed) for bounds check. A value like 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF is stored as uint64 max but compared as int64 -1, which is < cookie_num, bypassing the check\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F016\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"msg array (0x4060, 32x8=256 bytes) and msg_size array (0x4160, 32x4=128 bytes) are adjacent in .bss with no gap. msg ends at 0x4160, msg_size starts at 0x4160\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F017\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"msg_size ends at 0x41e0, urandom is at 0x41e0, cookie_num at 0x41e8. All in .bss, contiguous layout.\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F018\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"alarm(60) called in init(), process will receive SIGALRM after 60 seconds\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F019\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie swap does not zero msg[cookie_num-1] after copying to msg[random_index]. If random_index != cookie_num-1, the old slot retains a copy of the pointer (now freed).\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F020\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"With OOB index in edit_cookie/read_cookie, msg[index] can read from addresses beyond msg array, including msg_size, urandom, cookie_num, or other .bss/.data regions, depending on the index value\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY+ELF\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"id\":\"U001\",\"description\":\"Whether the OOB index in edit_cookie can reach a controllable pointer value (e.g., if msg[OOB] reads from msg_size which contains user-controlled values, that value interpreted as a pointer could be written to)\",\"reason\":\"Depends on exact index value and what global variable is hit. S2 needs to trace the data flow.\"},{\"id\":\"U002\",\"description\":\"Whether eat_cookie leaves a dangling pointer that can be exploited (UAF). The swap overwrites msg[random_index] but msg[cookie_num-1] retains the old value if random_index != cookie_num-1\",\"reason\":\"The freed pointer at msg[cookie_num-1] is at index cookie_num-1 which is now out of the valid range (cookie_num was decremented). edit/read check index < cookie_num, so the dangling slot is not directly accessible without OOB.\"},{\"id\":\"U003\",\"description\":\"Whether the OOB read in read_cookie can leak useful addresses (heap, libc, stack) for bypassing ASLR\",\"reason\":\"Depends on what pointer value exists at the OOB location in .bss/.data. S2 needs to determine what values are stored adjacent to msg.\"},{\"id\":\"U004\",\"description\":\"Whether msg_size OOB read in edit_cookie can yield a large value for the read length, amplifying the write primitive\",\"reason\":\"msg_size is int32 array; OOB read of 4 bytes from adjacent globals (urandom FILE*, cookie_num) could yield large sizes. S2 needs to verify.\"},{\"id\":\"U005\",\"description\":\"Whether seccomp or other sandbox is active\",\"reason\":\"No seccomp-related symbols found in binary, but runtime sandbox not confirmed.\"}],\"raw\":{\"elf\":\"ELF 64-bit LSB pie executable, x86-64, dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, not stripped. Entry=0x1110. 11 program headers, 29 sections.\",\"sections\":\".text@0x1110(0x891,R-E) .rodata@0x2000(0x266,R--) .data@0x4000(0x10,RW-) .bss@0x4020(0x1d0,RW-) .got@0x3f58(0xa8,RW-) .plt@0x1020(0xe0,R-E) .init_array@0x3d58(0x8,RW-) .fini_array@0x3d60(0x8,RW-)\",\"symbols\":\"Key: msg@0x4060(256B) msg_size@0x4160(128B) cookie_num@0x41e8(8B) urandom@0x41e0(8B) stdout@0x4020 stdin@0x4030 stderr@0x4040. Functions: init@0x1232 create_cookie@0x1399 eat_cookie@0x14e1 edit_cookie@0x1628 read_cookie@0x1751 menu@0x17fd main@0x187f _abort@0x11f5\",\"disassembly\":\"edit_cookie: scanf(\\\"%llu\\\",&idx) at 0x1671; cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x1692 (signed check); msg_size[idx] read at 0x16b6; msg[idx] deref at 0x16d2; read(0,msg[idx],msg_size[idx]-1) at 0x16de. read_cookie: scanf(\\\"%llu\\\",&idx) at 0x179a; cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x17bb (signed check); msg[idx] deref at 0x17ce; printf(\\\"%s\\\",msg[idx]) at 0x17e1. eat_cookie: fread 1 byte from urandom at 0x152b; idx=byte%cookie_num at 0x153e; free(msg[idx]) at 0x158e; swap msg[cookie_num-1]->msg[idx] at 0x15c6; cookie_num-- at 0x160a.\"}},\"vulnerability_report\":{\"schema\":\"VulnerabilityReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"total_bugs\":3,\"high_severity\":2,\"medium_severity\":1,\"low_severity\":0},\"vulnerabilities\":[{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"title\":\"OOB_SIGNEDNESS_MISMATCH_IN_EDIT_COOKIE\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie reads the user-supplied index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") as an unsigned 64-bit integer (uint64), but the bounds check at 0x1681-0x1684 uses 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' which is a SIGNED less-than comparison. A large unsigned value such as 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF is stored as uint64 max but interpreted as int64 -1 in the signed comparison. Since -1 < cookie_num (e.g., 5), the check passes. The attacker-controlled index is then used to compute msg[idx] (at 0x16c3-0x16d2, using lea rcx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+msg]; mov rax,[rcx+rax]) and msg_size[idx] (at 0x16a7-0x16b6, using lea rdx,[rax*4]; lea rax,[rip+msg_size]; mov eax,[rdx+rax]). With a negative/large index, these accesses read from memory before the msg and msg_size arrays (OOB). The OOB-read value from msg[idx] is treated as a heap pointer and used as the destination buffer for read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1), and the OOB-read value from msg_size[idx] determines the write length.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg (0x4060, pointer array, 32 elements)\",\"msg_size (0x4160, int32 array, 32 elements)\",\"urandom (0x41e0, FILE*)\",\"cookie_num (0x41e8, int64)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"edit_index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") in edit_cookie\",\"entry_function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker provides menu choice 3 → edit_cookie called\",\"scanf(\\\"%llu\\\", &idx) at 0x1671 reads unsigned 64-bit index into [rbp-0x10]\",\"At 0x1681: cmp rdx(idx), rax(cookie_num) — rdx holds the unsigned index value\",\"At 0x1684: jl 0x1692 — SIGNED less-than; 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF interpreted as -1 passes (-1 < 5)\",\"At 0x16a7-0x16b6: msg_size[idx] computed as *(int32*)(0x4160 + idx*4) — OOB read of 4 bytes from .bss before msg_size if idx is negative\",\"At 0x16bc: sub eax,0x1 — subtracts 1 from the OOB-read size value\",\"At 0x16c3-0x16d2: msg[idx] computed as *(ptr*)(0x4060 + idx*8) — OOB read of 8 bytes from .bss before msg if idx is negative\",\"At 0x16d6-0x16de: read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) — writes attacker data to the OOB-read pointer with OOB-read length\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Index should be validated as an unsigned value in range [0, cookie_num-1] using an unsigned comparison (jb/jae) or by checking both lower and upper bounds explicitly.\",\"actual\":\"Index is read as unsigned uint64 via %llu but compared using jl (signed less-than). Only upper bound is checked; no lower bound check exists. Negative-valued signed indices bypass the check.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check on index. Signed comparison (jl) used where unsigned comparison (jb) is required. The %llu format reads the full unsigned 64-bit range, but jl treats the value as signed, allowing values >= 0x8000000000000000 (negative in signed interpretation) to pass.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"OOB array read of pointer + OOB array read of size + heap write via read()\",\"address\":\"0x16d2 (msg[idx] dereference) and 0x16de (read call)\",\"expression\":\"read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) where idx is attacker-controlled and bypasses bounds check\",\"target\":\"msg[idx] yields a pointer read from OOB .bss memory; that pointer is used as write destination\",\"size\":\"msg_size[idx]-1, where msg_size[idx] is read from OOB .bss memory (4 bytes)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"With a negative index, msg[idx] reads 8 bytes from .bss before the msg array (e.g., from stdout@0x4020, stdin@0x4030, stderr@0x4040, or completed.0@0x4048). Similarly, msg_size[idx] reads 4 bytes from OOB .bss. These OOB reads expose memory contents that were not intended to be accessed as array elements.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The OOB-read pointer from msg[idx] is used as the destination buffer for read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1). If the OOB-read pointer value points to a valid writable memory region, attacker-controlled data from stdin is written to that location. The write length is determined by msg_size[idx]-1, which is also read from OOB memory and could be a large or unexpected value.\"},{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The combination of OOB pointer read and subsequent write to that pointer can corrupt memory at arbitrary writable locations pointed to by values stored in .bss (e.g., FILE* pointers like stdout/stdin/stderr, or other global variables).\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the OOB-read pointer value is NULL or points to unmapped memory, the read() call will attempt to write to an invalid address, causing SIGSEGV.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1671\",\"description\":\"scanf called with format string at 0x21c4 which is \\\"%llu\\\" (confirmed in .rodata), reading an unsigned 64-bit integer into [rbp-0x10]\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684\",\"description\":\"Bounds check: 'cmp rdx,rax' (rdx=user index, rax=cookie_num) followed by 'jl 0x1692'. jl is a signed less-than jump. A value like 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (uint64 max, int64 -1) passes because -1 < 5 in signed comparison.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a7-0x16b6\",\"description\":\"msg_size[idx] access: 'lea rdx,[rax*4+0x0]; lea rax,[rip+0x2aaa] (#4160 msg_size); mov eax,DWORD PTR [rdx+rax*1]'. With negative idx, this reads 4 bytes from before msg_size in .bss.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16c3-0x16d2\",\"description\":\"msg[idx] access: 'lea rcx,[rax*8+0x0]; lea rax,[rip+0x298e] (#4060 msg); mov rax,QWORD PTR [rcx+rax*1]'. With negative idx, this reads 8 bytes from before msg in .bss, yielding a pointer value.\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16d6-0x16de\",\"description\":\"read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1): the OOB-read pointer (from E004) is passed as the buffer argument to read(), and the OOB-read size (from E003, minus 1) is the length. Attacker data from stdin is written to the OOB pointer location.\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4060-0x41f0\",\"description\":\"BSS layout: msg@0x4060(256B), msg_size@0x4160(128B), urandom@0x41e0(8B), cookie_num@0x41e8(8B). Before msg@0x4060: completed.0@0x4048, stderr@0x4040, stdin@0x4030, stdout@0x4020. A negative index like -1 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) accesses msg[-1] = *(0x4060-8) = *(0x4058), which is in the gap between completed.0 and msg, or could overlap with .data section values.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm what pointer value exists at the OOB-accessed .bss location for specific negative index values\",\"Verify whether the OOB-read msg_size value yields a usable write length (not 0 or negative after -1)\",\"Dynamically confirm that a specific negative index value passes the jl check and reaches the read() call\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-002\",\"title\":\"OOB_SIGNEDNESS_MISMATCH_IN_READ_COOKIE\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"read_cookie reads the user-supplied index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") as an unsigned 64-bit integer (uint64), but the bounds check at 0x17aa-0x17ad uses 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' which is a SIGNED less-than comparison. A large unsigned value such as 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF is stored as uint64 max but interpreted as int64 -1 in the signed comparison. Since -1 < cookie_num (e.g., 5), the check passes. The attacker-controlled index is then used to compute msg[idx] (at 0x17bb-0x17ce, using lea rdx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+msg]; mov rax,[rdx+rax]). The OOB-read value is treated as a pointer and passed to printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[idx]) at 0x17e1, which dereferences and outputs the string at that pointer address.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg (0x4060, pointer array, 32 elements)\",\"urandom (0x41e0, FILE*)\",\"cookie_num (0x41e8, int64)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"view_index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") in read_cookie\",\"entry_function\":\"read_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker provides menu choice 4 → read_cookie called\",\"scanf(\\\"%llu\\\", &idx) at 0x179a reads unsigned 64-bit index into [rbp-0x10]\",\"At 0x17a3: mov rax,[rip+cookie_num] loads cookie_num into rax\",\"At 0x17aa: cmp rdx(idx), rax(cookie_num) — rdx holds the unsigned index value\",\"At 0x17ad: jl 0x17bb — SIGNED less-than; 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF interpreted as -1 passes (-1 < 5)\",\"At 0x17bb-0x17ce: msg[idx] computed as *(ptr*)(0x4060 + idx*8) — OOB read of 8 bytes from .bss\",\"At 0x17d2-0x17e1: printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[idx]) — the OOB-read pointer is dereferenced and its string content is printed to stdout\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Index should be validated as an unsigned value in range [0, cookie_num-1] using an unsigned comparison (jb/jae) or by checking both lower and upper bounds explicitly.\",\"actual\":\"Index is read as unsigned uint64 via %llu but compared using jl (signed less-than). Only upper bound is checked; no lower bound check exists. Negative-valued signed indices bypass the check.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check on index. Signed comparison (jl) used where unsigned comparison (jb) is required. The %llu format reads the full unsigned 64-bit range, but jl treats the value as signed, allowing values >= 0x8000000000000000 (negative in signed interpretation) to pass.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"OOB array read of pointer + pointer dereference via printf\",\"address\":\"0x17ce (msg[idx] dereference) and 0x17e1 (printf call)\",\"expression\":\"printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[idx]) where idx is attacker-controlled and bypasses bounds check\",\"target\":\"msg[idx] yields a pointer read from OOB .bss memory; that pointer is dereferenced by printf\",\"size\":\"unbounded (printf %s reads until null terminator)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"With a negative index, msg[idx] reads 8 bytes from .bss before the msg array (e.g., from stdout@0x4020, stdin@0x4030, stderr@0x4040). These OOB reads expose pointer values stored in global variables.\"},{\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The OOB-read pointer value is passed to printf(\\\"%s\\\", ptr), which dereferences it and outputs the string content at that address to stdout. If the OOB-read pointer points to heap memory (e.g., a strdup'd cookie string) or libc internal structures, memory content at that address is leaked to the attacker. This could expose heap addresses, libc addresses, or other sensitive data.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the OOB-read pointer value is NULL, invalid, or points to unmapped memory, the printf %s dereference will cause SIGSEGV.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x179a\",\"description\":\"scanf called with format string at 0x21c4 which is \\\"%llu\\\" (confirmed in .rodata), reading an unsigned 64-bit integer into [rbp-0x10]\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"Bounds check: 'cmp rdx,rax' (rdx=user index, rax=cookie_num) followed by 'jl 0x17bb'. jl is a signed less-than jump. A value like 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (uint64 max, int64 -1) passes because -1 < 5 in signed comparison.\"},{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb-0x17ce\",\"description\":\"msg[idx] access: 'lea rdx,[rax*8+0x0]; lea rax,[rip+0x2892] (#4060 msg); mov rax,QWORD PTR [rdx+rax*1]'. With negative idx, this reads 8 bytes from before msg in .bss, yielding a pointer value.\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17d2-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[idx]): the OOB-read pointer (from E009) is passed as the string argument to printf at 0x17e1. printf dereferences this pointer and outputs all bytes until a null terminator, leaking memory content.\"},{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4020-0x4060\",\"description\":\"BSS layout before msg@0x4060: stdout@0x4020(8B FILE*), stdin@0x4030(8B FILE*), stderr@0x4040(8B FILE*), completed.0@0x4048(1B). A negative index like -1 accesses msg[-1] = *(0x4058), which is in the padding/gap area. Index -8 would access *(0x4060-64) = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* pointer, which when passed to printf %s would dereference the FILE* and leak its contents.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm what pointer value exists at the OOB-accessed .bss location for specific negative index values\",\"Dynamically confirm that a specific negative index value passes the jl check and reaches the printf call\",\"Determine whether the leaked memory content includes useful address information (heap, libc) for ASLR bypass\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-003\",\"title\":\"DANGLING_POINTER_IN_EAT_COOKIE_SWAP\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Use-After-Free\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie frees msg[random_index] at 0x158e, then performs a swap: it copies msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[random_index] (at 0x15c6) and copies msg_size[cookie_num-1] into msg_size[random_index] (at 0x15fc). However, the old slot msg[cookie_num-1] is NOT zeroed after the copy. When random_index != cookie_num-1 (the common case when more than one cookie exists), msg[cookie_num-1] still holds the same pointer value that was just copied to msg[random_index]. But the object at that pointer has been freed. After cookie_num is decremented (0x160a), the dangling pointer at index cookie_num (the new, decremented value) is now outside the valid range [0, cookie_num-1]. However, due to BUG-001/BUG-002 (signedness mismatch), an attacker can access this out-of-range slot using a negative or large unsigned index, reaching the dangling pointer and dereferencing it (UAF read via read_cookie printf, or UAF write via edit_cookie read).\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"eat_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg (0x4060, pointer array)\",\"msg_size (0x4160, int32 array)\",\"cookie_num (0x41e8, int64)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"random_index from /dev/urandom (1 byte % cookie_num) in eat_cookie; then OOB index via scanf %llu in edit_cookie or read_cookie\",\"entry_function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"eat_cookie: fread 1 byte from /dev/urandom at 0x152b\",\"random_index = byte % cookie_num at 0x153e (idiv)\",\"free(msg[random_index]) at 0x158e — object is freed\",\"Swap: msg[cookie_num-1] → msg[random_index] at 0x15c6 (mov QWORD PTR [rcx+rdx*1], rax)\",\"Swap: msg_size[cookie_num-1] → msg_size[random_index] at 0x15fc (mov DWORD PTR [rcx+rdx*1], eax)\",\"cookie_num-- at 0x160a\",\"msg[old_cookie_num-1] (now at index cookie_num, outside valid range) still holds the freed pointer — dangling pointer\",\"Attacker uses edit_cookie or read_cookie with OOB index (via BUG-001/BUG-002 signedness bypass) to access the dangling slot\",\"edit_cookie: read(0, msg[dangling_idx], msg_size[dangling_idx]-1) — UAF write to freed memory\",\"read_cookie: printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[dangling_idx]) — UAF read from freed memory\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"After freeing msg[random_index] and swapping, the old slot msg[cookie_num-1] should be set to NULL to prevent any dangling pointer from remaining accessible.\",\"actual\":\"The swap copies msg[cookie_num-1] to msg[random_index] but does not zero msg[cookie_num-1]. The freed pointer remains in the msg array at index cookie_num-1 (which becomes index cookie_num after decrement).\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing NULL-ification of msg[cookie_num-1] after the swap copy. The dangling pointer persists in the array and is reachable via the OOB signedness bypass in edit_cookie/read_cookie.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"free(msg[random_index]) followed by swap without zeroing old slot, then later dereference via OOB index\",\"address\":\"0x158e (free), 0x15c6 (swap copy), 0x160a (cookie_num decrement)\",\"expression\":\"free(msg[idx]); msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1]; /* msg[cookie_num-1] NOT zeroed */ cookie_num--;\",\"target\":\"msg[cookie_num-1] (old index, now dangling after decrement)\",\"size\":\"8 bytes (pointer)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the attacker accesses the dangling slot via read_cookie's OOB signedness bypass, printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[dangling_idx]) dereferences the freed pointer. If the freed heap chunk has been reallocated or contains metadata, its content is leaked to stdout.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the attacker accesses the dangling slot via edit_cookie's OOB signedness bypass, read(0, msg[dangling_idx], msg_size[dangling_idx]-1) writes attacker-controlled data to the freed heap chunk. This can corrupt heap metadata or overlapping allocations.\"},{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"Writing to a freed heap chunk can corrupt the heap allocator's internal structures (chunk headers, free list pointers), potentially causing subsequent allocation/deallocation operations to behave incorrectly.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the freed pointer has been reclaimed by the allocator and the chunk metadata is corrupted by the write, subsequent heap operations may trigger allocator assertions or SIGSEGV.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e\",\"description\":\"free(msg[random_index]) is called. The heap object at the pointer stored in msg[random_index] is freed.\"},{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1593-0x15c6\",\"description\":\"Swap operation: 'mov rax,[rip+cookie_num]; sub rax,0x1' computes cookie_num-1, then 'lea rcx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+msg]; mov rax,[rcx+rax]' reads msg[cookie_num-1], then 'movsxd rdx,edx; lea rcx,[rdx*8]; lea rdx,[rip+msg]; mov QWORD PTR [rcx+rdx*1],rax' writes the value of msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[random_index]. The source slot msg[cookie_num-1] is NOT zeroed.\"},{\"id\":\"E014\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15ff-0x160a\",\"description\":\"cookie_num is decremented: 'mov rax,[rip+cookie_num]; sub rax,0x1; mov [rip+cookie_num],rax'. After this, the old msg[cookie_num-1] slot is at index cookie_num (new value), which is outside the valid range [0, cookie_num-1].\"},{\"id\":\"E015\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e-0x160a\",\"description\":\"When random_index != cookie_num-1 (which occurs whenever there is more than one cookie and the random byte does not map to the last index), the freed pointer is copied to msg[random_index] (which is valid and in-use) AND remains in msg[cookie_num-1] (which becomes the dangling slot after decrement). The dangling slot is accessible via the OOB signedness bypass in edit_cookie (BUG-001) or read_cookie (BUG-002).\"},{\"id\":\"E016\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1530-0x1541\",\"description\":\"random_index = (int32)fread_result % cookie_num. The index is derived from /dev/urandom, so it is not directly attacker-controlled. However, by repeatedly calling eat_cookie and creating cookies, the attacker can influence which slot becomes dangling and when. The attacker controls the timing and sequence of eat_cookie/create_cookie calls.\"}],\"confidence\":\"MEDIUM\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm that the dangling pointer at msg[old_cookie_num-1] is reachable via the OOB signedness bypass with a specific index value\",\"Verify that the freed heap chunk has not been reallocated before the UAF access occurs\",\"Dynamically confirm the UAF read/write by observing memory content changes at the freed chunk address\",\"Determine whether the random_index can be influenced indirectly (e.g., by controlling cookie_num to constrain the modulo result)\"]}],\"non_vulnerabilities\":[{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"description\":\"create_cookie reads message_size via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") and checks cookie_num <= 0x1f (jle at 0x13bb) and message_size <= 0x100 (jle at 0x13fc). It uses calloc(message_size, 1) for allocation and read(0, buf, message_size) for input.\",\"reason\":\"The size check uses jle (signed) but message_size is read via %lld (signed), so the signed comparison is consistent. The upper bound 0x100 (256) is reasonable. calloc zero-initializes the buffer. cookie_num is bounded to 31. No integer overflow in calloc(size,1) since size <= 256. The read length equals the allocation size, so no heap overflow. No vulnerability identified in this function.\"},{\"function\":\"main\",\"description\":\"main reads menu_choice via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") and dispatches to functions 1-5 using cmp+je/jne chain.\",\"reason\":\"The menu choice is compared against small constants (1-5) using exact equality (cmp+je). No array indexing or memory operation is performed on the menu choice value. Invalid choices simply loop back to the menu. No vulnerability in the dispatch logic.\"},{\"function\":\"init\",\"description\":\"init sets up buffering, alarm(60), opens /dev/urandom, and pre-creates 5 cookies via strdup with fixed sizes.\",\"reason\":\"All operations use fixed strings and sizes. The fopen result is checked for NULL. strdup allocations are stored at fixed indices msg[0..4]. No user input is processed. No vulnerability identified.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether the OOB index in edit_cookie can reach a specific .bss location containing a controllable or useful pointer value (e.g., a heap pointer from stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* structures)\",\"reason\":\"The exact pointer values stored in stdout@0x4020, stdin@0x4030, stderr@0x4040 depend on runtime libc initialization. These are FILE* pointers to libc-internal structures. Whether dereferencing them as write destinations yields useful memory corruption depends on the FILE structure layout and whether the write target falls in writable memory.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S3 dynamic verification: run the binary, set breakpoint at edit_cookie's read() call (0x16de), provide OOB index, and inspect the pointer value and write destination.\"},{\"description\":\"Whether the OOB read in read_cookie can leak addresses useful for ASLR bypass\",\"reason\":\"The leaked content depends on what the OOB-read pointer points to and what string data exists at that address. If the pointer is a FILE* (e.g., stdout), printf %s would output the FILE structure's internal buffer content, which may contain libc addresses. But this depends on runtime state.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S3 dynamic verification: run the binary, provide OOB index to read_cookie, and inspect the output for address leaks.\"},{\"description\":\"Whether the UAF in eat_cookie can be reliably triggered given the random index\",\"reason\":\"The random_index is derived from /dev/urandom, so the attacker cannot directly control which slot is freed. However, by controlling the sequence of eat_cookie and create_cookie calls, the attacker can influence cookie_num and thus the modulo result. The feasibility of reliably reaching the dangling slot via the OOB signedness bypass needs dynamic confirmation.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S3 dynamic verification: run the binary through multiple eat_cookie/create_cookie cycles, then use OOB index in edit_cookie/read_cookie to access the dangling slot.\"}]},\"primitive_reports\":[],\"target_bug\":\"\",\"bug_reports\":[],\"impact_reports\":[],\"strategy_reports\":[],\"final_exploit_path\":\"\",\"final_report\":{}}",
  "vulnerability_report": {
    "schema": "VulnerabilityReport",
    "version": "1.0",
    "status": "COMPLETED",
    "summary": {
      "total_bugs": 3,
      "high_severity": 2,
      "medium_severity": 1,
      "low_severity": 0
    },
    "vulnerabilities": [
      {
        "bug_id": "BUG-001",
        "title": "OOB_SIGNEDNESS_MISMATCH_IN_EDIT_COOKIE",
        "root_cause": {
          "type": "Signedness Error",
          "description": "edit_cookie reads the user-supplied index via scanf(\"%llu\") as an unsigned 64-bit integer (uint64), but the bounds check at 0x1681-0x1684 uses 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' which is a SIGNED less-than comparison. A large unsigned value such as 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF is stored as uint64 max but interpreted as int64 -1 in the signed comparison. Since -1 < cookie_num (e.g., 5), the check passes. The attacker-controlled index is then used to compute msg[idx] (at 0x16c3-0x16d2, using lea rcx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+msg]; mov rax,[rcx+rax]) and msg_size[idx] (at 0x16a7-0x16b6, using lea rdx,[rax*4]; lea rax,[rip+msg_size]; mov eax,[rdx+rax]). With a negative/large index, these accesses read from memory before the msg and msg_size arrays (OOB). The OOB-read value from msg[idx] is treated as a heap pointer and used as the destination buffer for read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1), and the OOB-read value from msg_size[idx] determines the write length."
        },
        "affected_functions": [
          "edit_cookie"
        ],
        "affected_objects": [
          "msg (0x4060, pointer array, 32 elements)",
          "msg_size (0x4160, int32 array, 32 elements)",
          "urandom (0x41e0, FILE*)",
          "cookie_num (0x41e8, int64)"
        ],
        "attack_surface": {
          "input": "edit_index via scanf(\"%llu\") in edit_cookie",
          "entry_function": "edit_cookie",
          "data_flow": [
            "Attacker provides menu choice 3 → edit_cookie called",
            "scanf(\"%llu\", &idx) at 0x1671 reads unsigned 64-bit index into [rbp-0x10]",
            "At 0x1681: cmp rdx(idx), rax(cookie_num) — rdx holds the unsigned index value",
            "At 0x1684: jl 0x1692 — SIGNED less-than; 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF interpreted as -1 passes (-1 < 5)",
            "At 0x16a7-0x16b6: msg_size[idx] computed as *(int32*)(0x4160 + idx*4) — OOB read of 4 bytes from .bss before msg_size if idx is negative",
            "At 0x16bc: sub eax,0x1 — subtracts 1 from the OOB-read size value",
            "At 0x16c3-0x16d2: msg[idx] computed as *(ptr*)(0x4060 + idx*8) — OOB read of 8 bytes from .bss before msg if idx is negative",
            "At 0x16d6-0x16de: read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) — writes attacker data to the OOB-read pointer with OOB-read length"
          ]
        },
        "security_check": {
          "expected": "Index should be validated as an unsigned value in range [0, cookie_num-1] using an unsigned comparison (jb/jae) or by checking both lower and upper bounds explicitly.",
          "actual": "Index is read as unsigned uint64 via %llu but compared using jl (signed less-than). Only upper bound is checked; no lower bound check exists. Negative-valued signed indices bypass the check.",
          "missing_or_incorrect": "Missing lower-bound check on index. Signed comparison (jl) used where unsigned comparison (jb) is required. The %llu format reads the full unsigned 64-bit range, but jl treats the value as signed, allowing values >= 0x8000000000000000 (negative in signed interpretation) to pass."
        },
        "vulnerable_operation": {
          "operation": "OOB array read of pointer + OOB array read of size + heap write via read()",
          "address": "0x16d2 (msg[idx] dereference) and 0x16de (read call)",
          "expression": "read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) where idx is attacker-controlled and bypasses bounds check",
          "target": "msg[idx] yields a pointer read from OOB .bss memory; that pointer is used as write destination",
          "size": "msg_size[idx]-1, where msg_size[idx] is read from OOB .bss memory (4 bytes)"
        },
        "impact": [
          {
            "type": "OOB_READ",
            "status": "CANDIDATE",
            "reason": "With a negative index, msg[idx] reads 8 bytes from .bss before the msg array (e.g., from stdout@0x4020, stdin@0x4030, stderr@0x4040, or completed.0@0x4048). Similarly, msg_size[idx] reads 4 bytes from OOB .bss. These OOB reads expose memory contents that were not intended to be accessed as array elements."
          },
          {
            "type": "OOB_WRITE",
            "status": "CANDIDATE",
            "reason": "The OOB-read pointer from msg[idx] is used as the destination buffer for read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1). If the OOB-read pointer value points to a valid writable memory region, attacker-controlled data from stdin is written to that location. The write length is determined by msg_size[idx]-1, which is also read from OOB memory and could be a large or unexpected value."
          },
          {
            "type": "MEMORY_CORRUPTION",
            "status": "CANDIDATE",
            "reason": "The combination of OOB pointer read and subsequent write to that pointer can corrupt memory at arbitrary writable locations pointed to by values stored in .bss (e.g., FILE* pointers like stdout/stdin/stderr, or other global variables)."
          },
          {
            "type": "CRASH",
            "status": "CANDIDATE",
            "reason": "If the OOB-read pointer value is NULL or points to unmapped memory, the read() call will attempt to write to an invalid address, causing SIGSEGV."
          }
        ],
