)=stdout FILE* (0x7ffff7fa75c0).\",\"necessity\":\"The write target must be a valid writable address for read() to succeed\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"msg_size[idx] must yield a valid positive read length. For idx=-4, msg_size[-4]=*(0x4150)=0 (read size=-1, fails). For idx=-8, msg_size[-8]=*(0x4140)=0 (fails). Most OOB indices have msg_size=0.\",\"necessity\":\"Without valid size, read() returns -1 and no write occurs\",\"verified\":false}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-4] = *(0x4060-32) = *(0x4040) = stderr FILE* = 0x7ffff7fa74e0\",\"alias\":\"msg[-4] aliases stderr FILE* pointer\",\"implication\":\"read(0, stderr_ptr, msg_size[-4]-1) would write to libc FILE structure. But msg_size[-4]=0, so read size=-1, fails.\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-8] = *(0x4060-64) = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* = 0x7ffff7fa75c0\",\"alias\":\"msg[-8] aliases stdout FILE* pointer\",\"implication\":\"read(0, stdout_ptr, msg_size[-8]-1) would write to libc FILE structure. But msg_size[-8]=0, so read size=-1, fails.\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Test various negative indices to find one where both msg[idx] is a valid writable pointer AND msg_size[idx] is a positive value. GDB: set breakpoint at read() call, inspect rsi (target) and rdx (size).\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, ASLR disabled, GDB\",\"run_command\":\"gdb: b *edit_cookie+0xb6; run with OOB index; inspect rsi/rdx\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"rsi = valid writable address\",\"rdx = positive value (not -1)\",\"read() returns > 0\",\"Memory at rsi changed after read()\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE/ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"FILE* pointers (stdout/stdin/stderr) contain libc addresses which change with ASLR. The write target is a libc address, not directly attacker-controlled.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Does not prevent writing to libc FILE structures (which are in writable libc memory).\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16c3-0x16d2\",\"description\":\"msg[idx] with negative idx reads from .bss before msg array. For idx=-4, reads stderr FILE* (0x7ffff7fa74e0). For idx=-8, reads stdout FILE* (0x7ffff7fa75c0).\"},{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB: For idx=-4, at read() call: rsi=0x7ffff7fa74e0 (stderr FILE*), rdx=-1 (msg_size[-4]=0, size=0-1=-1). read() returns -1 (EINVAL). No write occurs. For idx=-8: rsi=0x7ffff7fa75c0 (stdout FILE*), rdx=-1. Same failure.\"},{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4020-0x4060\",\"description\":\"GDB dump of .bss before msg: stdout@0x4020=0x7ffff7fa75c0, stdin@0x4030=0x7ffff7fa68e0, stderr@0x4040=0x7ffff7fa74e0. All FILE* pointers. Corresponding msg_size OOB values are all 0 (padding/completed.0), causing read size=-1.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"Tested idx=-4 (stderr FILE*) and idx=-8 (stdout FILE*): both have msg_size[idx]=0, causing read size=-1, read() returns -1 (EINVAL). No write occurs. The direct OOB write via FILE* pointers fails because the corresponding msg_size OOB values are 0. Only idx=-11 (via __dso_handle + msg[26] high bytes alias) yields a valid size, which is the two-step ARB_WRITE (PRIM-001).\"},\"confidence\":\"MEDIUM\",\"notes\":\"Direct OOB write via FILE* pointers (stdout/stdin/stderr at idx -8/-6/-4) fails because msg_size at those OOB indices is 0 (padding bytes in .bss), making read size=-1. The only viable OOB write path is through idx=-11 (__dso_handle self-referencing pointer + msg[26] high 4 bytes alias), which is the two-step ARB_WRITE (PRIM-001). This primitive remains CANDIDATE as a RELATIVE_WRITE because the write target (FILE* pointer) is not attacker-controlled — it's whatever happens to be stored in .bss. Even if a valid size were found, the target would be a fixed libc address, not an attacker-chosen address.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-003\",\"type\":\"RELATIVE_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Same OOB signedness mismatch in edit_cookie. The OOB read of msg[idx] and msg_size[idx] from .bss before the msg array exposes memory contents (FILE* pointers, padding, __dso_handle value) that were not intended to be accessible as array elements.\",\"description\":\"OOB read: msg[idx] reads 8 bytes from .bss before msg, and msg_size[idx] reads 4 bytes. These values are used internally (as write destination and size) but are not directly output to the attacker. However, the OOB-read pointer value can be indirectly observed via crash behavior (fault_address reveals the pointer value).\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001: signedness mismatch in edit_cookie\",\"OOB negative index reads 8 bytes from .bss before msg array\",\"The read value is used as write destination (not directly output)\",\"RELATIVE_READ: OOB memory contents exposed via indirect observation (crash address, GDB)\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Read location (via OOB index selection)\",\"how\":\"Attacker chooses the negative index, which determines which .bss address is read\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":false},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"OOB index must pass signed bounds check (same as PRIM-001 PC-01)\",\"necessity\":\"Required to reach the OOB access\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"The OOB-read value must be observable. In edit_cookie, the value is used as write destination (not printed). Observation requires GDB or crash analysis.\",\"necessity\":\"Without observation, the read is silent and cannot be exploited as an info leak\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-11] = *(0x4008) = __dso_handle = 0x555555558008\",\"alias\":\"msg[-11] reads __dso_handle self-referencing pointer\",\"implication\":\"The OOB read exposes the PIE base address (0x555555558008 = PIE_base + 0x4008). This is a potential info leak for ASLR bypass.\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"GDB: set breakpoint at read() call in edit_cookie, provide OOB index, inspect rsi (which holds the OOB-read pointer value). This reveals what memory content was read.\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, ASLR disabled, GDB\",\"run_command\":\"gdb: b *edit_cookie+0xb6; run with OOB index; info registers rsi\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"rsi = OOB-read pointer value from .bss\",\"Different indices yield different .bss values (stdout, stdin, stderr, __dso_handle)\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE/ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"The OOB-read values (FILE* pointers, __dso_handle) contain PIE/libc addresses. With ASLR, these are randomized but still leakable via the OOB read.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16d2\",\"description\":\"GDB: At read() call with idx=-11, rsi=0x555555558008 (__dso_handle value). This is the OOB-read pointer from msg[-11]. With idx=-8, rsi=0x7ffff7fa75c0 (stdout FILE*). With idx=-4, rsi=0x7ffff7fa74e0 (stderr FILE*). Different indices read different .bss values.\"},{\"id\":\"E014\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4020-0x4060\",\"description\":\"BSS layout: stdout@0x4020, stdin@0x4030, stderr@0x4040, __dso_handle@0x4008. OOB indices -8/-6/-4/-11 read these values respectively.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"rsi=0x555555558008 (idx=-11), rsi=0x7ffff7fa75c0 (idx=-8), rsi=0x7ffff7fa74e0 (idx=-4)\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"GDB confirmed OOB read of .bss values via different negative indices. idx=-11 reads __dso_handle (0x555555558008, leaks PIE base). idx=-8 reads stdout FILE* (0x7ffff7fa75c0, leaks libc address). idx=-4 reads stderr FILE* (0x7ffff7fa74e0). The values are observable via GDB (rsi register at read() call) but not directly output to the attacker in edit_cookie (the value is used as write destination, not printed). For a direct info leak, read_cookie (BUG-002) would output the OOB-read pointer via printf.\"},\"confidence\":\"MEDIUM\",\"notes\":\"The OOB read in edit_cookie exposes .bss values (FILE* pointers, __dso_handle) but does not directly output them to the attacker. The values are used internally as write destination/size. Observation requires GDB or crash analysis (fault_address reveals the pointer value). This is RELATIVE_READ (not ARB_READ) because the read location is constrained to the .bss region before the msg array, determined by the negative index. For a direct info leak primitive, read_cookie (BUG-002) is more suitable as it outputs the OOB-read pointer via printf. The __dso_handle value (0x555555558008) leaks the PIE base, which is useful for ASLR bypass in the ARB_WRITE primitive (PRIM-001).\"}],\"non_primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-001\",\"candidate_type\":\"CRASH\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"When the OOB-read pointer is NULL (e.g., idx=-1 reads msg[-1]=*(0x4058)=0) or when msg_size[idx]=0 (read size=-1), the read() call fails or crashes. This is a side effect of the OOB access, not a useful primitive. The crash at fault_address=0x414141414141414a (Proof C) is used as evidence for ARB_WRITE, not as a standalone CRASH primitive.\"}],\"unresolved\":[]}],\"target_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"bug_reports\":[],\"impact_reports\":[],\"strategy_reports\":[],\"final_exploit_path\":\"\",\"final_report\":{}}",
